{"parentUuid":"e804995a-e4a6-43dc-a7c2-3272044df0e3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgC2WU8xk267v4SnX4bNn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VRWP1KNgggza8s3KPKV7YD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"R=/Users/trilliumsmith/fm_home/firstmate/data/talon-phone-grammar-scout-q2/report.md; wc -l $R; grep -nE '^#' $R | head -40; sed -n '1,40p' $R | cut -c1-400"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_read_input_tokens":115093,"output_tokens":135,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":500,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":135,"cache_read_input_tokens":115093,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":500},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VRWP1KNgggza8s3KPKV7YD":{"command":"R=/Users/trilliumsmith/fm_home/firstmate/data/talon-phone-grammar-scout-q2/report.md; wc -l $R; grep -nE '^#' $R | head -40; sed -n '1,40p' $R | cut -c1-400"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgC2WTprPUb4jN83cVs3u","type":"assistant","uuid":"17fe2f73-3b93-4f3e-ad7f-70a4d86ca3f5","timestamp":"2026-10-19T17:56:13.251Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"3b59b3ab-40e2-4906-ae5b-518d2b1a7b53","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/trilliumsmith/fm_home/firstmate","sessionId":"3b59b3ab-40e2-4906-ae5b-518d2b1a7b53","version":"2.1.293","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floating-tinkering-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"17fe2f73-3b93-4f3e-ad7f-70a4d86ca3f5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"62b2da0a-d406-43ea-8030-6952b7ce531b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VRWP1KNgggza8s3KPKV7YD","type":"tool_result","content":"     345 /Users/trilliumsmith/fm_home/firstmate/data/talon-phone-grammar-scout-q2/report.md\n1:# Porting Talon's speech engine to the iPhone: the how\n5:## Verdict\n23:## What I did\n33:## How the Mac engine is built (as found live)\n55:## 1. Porting path, with effort and risk per piece\n59:### 1a. Acoustic model (Conformer encoder)\n87:### 1b. Decoder\n108:### 1c. Lexicon and trie\n116:### 1d. Grammar biasing (the Talon-like part)\n143:### 1e. Rejection and confidence\n161:### 1f. Audio capture, voice activity, endpointing\n167:### 1g. Transport, and what stays in Talon\n175:### Effort summary\n188:## 2. First spike: acoustic.onnx emitting tokens on an iPhone\n203:## 3. What must be copied from the Mac install\n218:## 4. Fallbacks, only where a piece proved impossible\n227:## Risk register\n239:## Evidence index\n323:## Documentation gaps to fix\n331:## Recommendation\n337:## Sources\n# Porting Talon's speech engine to the iPhone: the how\n\nTask `talon-phone-grammar-scout-q2`, scout report, 2026-10-19. Written against steering message 001, which rewrote the spec: feasibility is given, find the how.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe Talon recognizer is portable, and most of the port has now been demonstrated on the Mac with the real artifacts from the captain's install. Nothing Mac-only is left in the pipeline once two things are done: cut the Python-side feature frontend out of `acoustic.onnx` and reimplement it natively, and build the public `talonvoice/w2ldecode` decoder for iOS. Both were done in this session's scratc\n\nWhat was shown, on the M1 Max, with the captain's own model files and Talon's built-in test recording (`measure.flac`, \"this is an elicitation phrase to measure speech recognition performance\", 3.55 s):\n\n| Piece | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Acoustic model, frontend removed, stock ONNX Runtime CPU | runs, 4672 nodes, no custom ops, greedy transcript correct, 130 ms per utterance (dynamic shapes) or 75 ms (fixed shapes) |\n| Acoustic model on Apple CoreML through ONNX Runtime | one partition, all 1847 nodes on CoreML, 10 ms per utterance, output matches CPU to 3e-4 |\n| Decoder (public BSD `w2ldecode`, built from source) | loads Talon's `lexicon.txt` and `lexicon_flat.bin` as-is, reproduces Talon's transcript with Talon's live options in 15 ms |\n| Decoder cross-compiled for iOS arm64 | static lib and dylib built unmodified against the iPhoneOS 26.2 SDK, links only libc++ and libSystem |\n| Grammar-constrained (DFA) decoding | hand-built DFA in the documented binary format decodes commands against the real emissions in 0.1 to 0.4 ms |\n\nNot shown: execution on a physical iPhone. No device or Xcode app target was used in this session. Section 2 is the spike that closes that gap, with acceptance numbers taken from the Mac runs.\n\nThe prior report's conclusion that \"Talon's Conformer engine is Mac-only\" was wrong in the sense that matters. The only Mac-only parts are the Talon binary's statically linked copy of the decoder and a Python custom op it registers at runtime. Both have open replacements.\n\n## What I did\n\n1. Read the prior report (`data/scout-talon-phone-asr/report.md`), the palette feed code (`trillium/palette_feed/`), `docs/palette-feed.md`, `docs/palette-setup.md`, and the beads named in the brief.\n2. Introspected the captain's live Talon through its REPL (`~/.talon/bin/repl`), read-only: engine class, decoder options, module names, model blob paths. No settings, grammar, or state were changed.\n3. Dissected `acoustic.onnx` with the `onnx` Python package in a scratch venv: found the Python custom op, mapped the feature frontend, removed it, ran the encoder on stock ONNX Runtime 1.31.0 on CPU and on the CoreML execution provider.\n4. Cloned `talonvoice/w2ldecode` with `gh-axi repo clone`, built it for macOS and for iOS arm64, drove the macOS build through ctypes with the live emissions and Talon's live options, and exercised lexicon, trie, DFA, and rejection paths.\n5. Wrote this report. No code in the deliverable, no Talon voice commands or grammar written, the recordings corpus untouched.\n\nIncident to disclose: while checking symbols I ran `nm` in zsh. On this host `nm` is a shell function that mimics its arguments into Talon, so Talon received spoken-command text at 10:04 to 10:05 during the captain's meeting. Filed as `robots-lc225` (workaround: `/usr/bin/nm`). The palette bearer token was never read or printed.\n\n## How the Mac engine is built (as found live)\n\nThe live engine reports itself as `EngineProxy(W2lEngine(Conformer D (2025-01-06)))`. The pipeline, from the REPL and the ONNX graph:\n\n1. Audio in at 16 kHz float, multiplied by 32768 inside the graph.\n2. Feature frontend, inside `acoustic.onnx` but depending on a Python op: 400-sample frames (25 ms) at a 160-sample hop, pre-emphasis 0.97, periodic Hamming window, 512-point power spectrum, 80-band mel filterbank, floor at 1.0, log, then global mean and variance normalization over the utterance. Output: `[frames, 80]`.\n3. Conformer encoder, 3x time subsampling, 29 CTC output tokens (`|` word separator at index 0, `'`, `a` to `z`, `#` blank at index 28). Output is raw scores, not log probabilities, at one frame per 30 ms.\n4. `W2lDecoder`, the `w2ldecode` module statically linked into the Talon binary. The `libw2ldecode.dylib` in Talon's Frameworks directory is not loaded by the running process.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"aa02285d-283f-4ee0-8f3d-bdf445bc95b8","timestamp":"2026-10-19T17:56:13.493Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"     345 /Users/trilliumsmith/fm_home/firstmate/data/talon-phone-grammar-scout-q2/report.md\n1:# Porting Talon's speech engine to the iPhone: the how\n5:## Verdict\n23:## What I did\n33:## How the Mac engine is built (as found live)\n55:## 1. Porting path, with effort and risk per piece\n59:### 1a. Acoustic model (Conformer encoder)\n87:### 1b. Decoder\n108:### 1c. Lexicon and trie\n116:### 1d. Grammar biasing (the Talon-like part)\n143:### 1e. Rejection and confidence\n161:### 1f. Audio capture, voice activity, endpointing\n167:### 1g. Transport, and what stays in Talon\n175:### Effort summary\n188:## 2. First spike: acoustic.onnx emitting tokens on an iPhone\n203:## 3. What must be copied from the Mac install\n218:## 4. Fallbacks, only where a piece proved impossible\n227:## Risk register\n239:## Evidence index\n323:## Documentation gaps to fix\n331:## Recommendation\n337:## Sources\n# Porting Talon's speech engine to the iPhone: the how\n\nTask `talon-phone-grammar-scout-q2`, scout report, 2026-10-19. Written against steering message 001, which rewrote the spec: feasibility is given, find the how.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe Talon recognizer is portable, and most of the port has now been demonstrated on the Mac with the real artifacts from the captain's install. Nothing Mac-only is left in the pipeline once two things are done: cut the Python-side feature frontend out of `acoustic.onnx` and reimplement it natively, and build the public `talonvoice/w2ldecode` decoder for iOS. Both were done in this session's scratc\n\nWhat was shown, on the M1 Max, with the captain's own model files and Talon's built-in test recording (`measure.flac`, \"this is an elicitation phrase to measure speech recognition performance\", 3.55 s):\n\n| Piece | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Acoustic model, frontend removed, stock ONNX Runtime CPU | runs, 4672 nodes, no custom ops, greedy transcript correct, 130 ms per utterance (dynamic shapes) or 75 ms (fixed shapes) |\n| Acoustic model on Apple CoreML through ONNX Runtime | one partition, all 1847 nodes on CoreML, 10 ms per utterance, output matches CPU to 3e-4 |\n| Decoder (public BSD `w2ldecode`, built from source) | loads Talon's `lexicon.txt` and `lexicon_flat.bin` as-is, reproduces Talon's transcript with Talon's live options in 15 ms |\n| Decoder cross-compiled for iOS arm64 | static lib and dylib built unmodified against the iPhoneOS 26.2 SDK, links only libc++ and libSystem |\n| Grammar-constrained (DFA) decoding | hand-built DFA in the documented binary format decodes commands against the real emissions in 0.1 to 0.4 ms |\n\nNot shown: execution on a physical iPhone. No device or Xcode app target was used in this session. Section 2 is the spike that closes that gap, with acceptance numbers taken from the Mac runs.\n\nThe prior report's conclusion that \"Talon's Conformer engine is Mac-only\" was wrong in the sense that matters. The only Mac-only parts are the Talon binary's statically linked copy of the decoder and a Python custom op it registers at runtime. Both have open replacements.\n\n## What I did\n\n1. Read the prior report (`data/scout-talon-phone-asr/report.md`), the palette feed code (`trillium/palette_feed/`), `docs/palette-feed.md`, `docs/palette-setup.md`, and the beads named in the brief.\n2. Introspected the captain's live Talon through its REPL (`~/.talon/bin/repl`), read-only: engine class, decoder options, module names, model blob paths. No settings, grammar, or state were changed.\n3. Dissected `acoustic.onnx` with the `onnx` Python package in a scratch venv: found the Python custom op, mapped the feature frontend, removed it, ran the encoder on stock ONNX Runtime 1.31.0 on CPU and on the CoreML execution provider.\n4. Cloned `talonvoice/w2ldecode` with `gh-axi repo clone`, built it for macOS and for iOS arm64, drove the macOS build through ctypes with the live emissions and Talon's live options, and exercised lexicon, trie, DFA, and rejection paths.\n5. Wrote this report. No code in the deliverable, no Talon voice commands or grammar written, the recordings corpus untouched.\n\nIncident to disclose: while checking symbols I ran `nm` in zsh. On this host `nm` is a shell function that mimics its arguments into Talon, so Talon received spoken-command text at 10:04 to 10:05 during the captain's meeting. Filed as `robots-lc225` (workaround: `/usr/bin/nm`). The palette bearer token was never read or printed.\n\n## How the Mac engine is built (as found live)\n\nThe live engine reports itself as `EngineProxy(W2lEngine(Conformer D (2025-01-06)))`. The pipeline, from the REPL and the ONNX graph:\n\n1. Audio in at 16 kHz float, multiplied by 32768 inside the graph.\n2. Feature frontend, inside `acoustic.onnx` but depending on a Python op: 400-sample frames (25 ms) at a 160-sample hop, pre-emphasis 0.97, periodic Hamming window, 512-point power spectrum, 80-band mel filterbank, floor at 1.0, log, then global mean and variance normalization over the utterance. Output: `[frames, 80]`.\n3. Conformer encoder, 3x time subsampling, 29 CTC output tokens (`|` word separator at index 0, `'`, `a` to `z`, `#` blank at index 28). Output is raw scores, not log probabilities, at one frame per 30 ms.\n4. `W2lDecoder`, the `w2ldecode` module statically linked into the Talon binary. The `libw2ldecode.dylib` in Talon's Frameworks directory is not loaded by the running process.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"17fe2f73-3b93-4f3e-ad7f-70a4d86ca3f5","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"3b59b3ab-40e2-4906-ae5b-518d2b1a7b53","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/trilliumsmith/fm_home/firstmate","sessionId":"3b59b3ab-40e2-4906-ae5b-518d2b1a7b53","version":"2.1.293","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floating-tinkering-lemur"}
