{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"3b59b3ab-40e2-4906-ae5b-518d2b1a7b53","prNumber":2,"prUrl":"https://github.com/trillium/forefront-backend/pull/2","prRepository":"trillium/forefront-backend","timestamp":"2026-10-19T17:56:16.734Z"}
{"parentUuid":"094ccb1c-2361-40cc-b849-4fdfb35dc0a2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"62b2da0a-d406-43ea-8030-6952b7ce531b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LW7uZ6VudkGo21viq5UryS","type":"tool_result","content":"### Effort summary\n\n| Piece | Days | Risk |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1a Acoustic model, frontend, ORT CoreML | 3 to 5 | low (correctness), medium (first-launch compile, padding) |\n| 1b Decoder cross-compile and wrapper | 1 to 2 | low |\n| 1c Lexicon and trie | under 1 | none seen |\n| 1d Grammar compiler | 4 to 7 | medium |\n| 1e Rejection | 1 plus tuning | low |\n| 1f Capture, VAD, endpointing | 2 to 3 | low |\n| 1g Transport | 1 | low |\n| Total | about 13 to 20 | |\n\n## 2. First spike: acoustic.onnx emitting tokens on an iPhone\n\nGoal: a bare iOS app that prints the 29-token greedy transcript of `measure.flac` on a physical iPhone. No grammar, no network, no UI beyond a label.\n\n1. Produce `encoder_only.onnx` on the Mac exactly as in 1a (remove the six frontend nodes, add input `525` `[frames, 80]`). Keep the dynamic model for step 5 and a `fixed353` basic-optimized copy for step 6. Export the mel matrix (constant `512`) and the tokens metadata to files.\n2. Convert `measure.flac` to 16 kHz mono PCM and also precompute the `[353, 80]` features on the Mac with the numpy frontend; bundle both. The features file lets the first run bypass the native frontend.\n3. Xcode app, deployment target iOS 15.1, `use_frameworks!`, pod `onnxruntime-objc` (or the C pod). Bundle `encoder_only.onnx`.\n4. Feed the precomputed features through the CPU EP, greedy-collapse the `(1,1,118,29)` output (argmax per frame, drop repeats, drop blank 28, map `|` to space). Acceptance: the label reads \"this is an elicitation phrase to measure speech recognition performance\", 118 frames. This is \"tokens on an iPhone\".\n5. Replace the bundled features with the native frontend over the bundled PCM. Acceptance: identical transcript, max feature difference against the Mac features under 1e-3.\n6. Load the `fixed353` model with the CoreML EP (`MLProgram`, `RequireStaticInputShapes=1`, `ModelCacheDirectory` set to the app's cache). Acceptance: one partition, identical transcript, per-utterance time measured and recorded alongside first-launch and cached-launch session creation times. Mac reference: 10 ms inference, 19 s first compile.\n7. Pad the PCM with 0.5 s and 2 s of silence, re-run with matching fixed buckets. Acceptance: identical transcript. This answers the padding question before the grammar work starts.\n8. Record the numbers in the project docs and stop. Decoder and grammar are the next spike, and their Mac baselines are in this report (15 ms lexicon decode, under 1 ms command DFA).\n\n## Risk register\n\n| Risk | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| CoreML first-launch compile of a 111 MB MLProgram is slow on the phone | likely, bounded | `ModelCacheDirectory`; compile buckets in the background on first run; CPU EP meanwhile |\n| Silence padding to a bucket changes accuracy | possible | measured in spike step 7; shrink bucket spacing or fall back to CPU EP dynamic graph |\n| Grammar compiler gets a rule-call or LMWORD_CTX detail wrong | possible | the C++ is short and readable; test each construct against the Mac decoder first with ctypes, as done here |\n| Talon's Python rejection formula differs from the proposed path-vs-greedy check | likely but harmless | the margin is two orders of magnitude; tune on real audio |\n| Non-open captures (`<user.letter>` and similar) not exported by the feed | certain today | export their rules; see doc gaps |\n| EULA wording on use outside Talon | unknown | read it; email support@talonvoice.com; personal use only |\n| ORT CoreML EP behavior changes across versions | low | pin the pod version; keep the CPU EP path |\n\n## Recommendation\n\nRun the section 2 spike next: it is 2 to 3 days, needs only the four files in section 3 and an ORT pod, and turns \"demonstrated on the Mac\" into \"runs on the phone\" with the acceptance numbers above. Follow it with the decoder-plus-DFA spike (the Mac harness already exists in spirit: emissions file in, text out), then the grammar compiler, which is the largest and most Talon-specific piece. Keep Talon as the authority on the Mac and ship phrases through the existing `/execute` endpoint; do not b\n\nWork that should ship from this task: none in this repo as code. Two follow-ups are concrete: amend the prior report's conclusion, and export non-open capture definitions from the palette feed (doc gap 2).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f3aaf0aa-3487-4b62-becd-5bc621c73956","timestamp":"2026-10-19T17:56:16.743Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"### Effort summary\n\n| Piece | Days | Risk |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1a Acoustic model, frontend, ORT CoreML | 3 to 5 | low (correctness), medium (first-launch compile, padding) |\n| 1b Decoder cross-compile and wrapper | 1 to 2 | low |\n| 1c Lexicon and trie | under 1 | none seen |\n| 1d Grammar compiler | 4 to 7 | medium |\n| 1e Rejection | 1 plus tuning | low |\n| 1f Capture, VAD, endpointing | 2 to 3 | low |\n| 1g Transport | 1 | low |\n| Total | about 13 to 20 | |\n\n## 2. First spike: acoustic.onnx emitting tokens on an iPhone\n\nGoal: a bare iOS app that prints the 29-token greedy transcript of `measure.flac` on a physical iPhone. No grammar, no network, no UI beyond a label.\n\n1. Produce `encoder_only.onnx` on the Mac exactly as in 1a (remove the six frontend nodes, add input `525` `[frames, 80]`). Keep the dynamic model for step 5 and a `fixed353` basic-optimized copy for step 6. Export the mel matrix (constant `512`) and the tokens metadata to files.\n2. Convert `measure.flac` to 16 kHz mono PCM and also precompute the `[353, 80]` features on the Mac with the numpy frontend; bundle both. The features file lets the first run bypass the native frontend.\n3. Xcode app, deployment target iOS 15.1, `use_frameworks!`, pod `onnxruntime-objc` (or the C pod). Bundle `encoder_only.onnx`.\n4. Feed the precomputed features through the CPU EP, greedy-collapse the `(1,1,118,29)` output (argmax per frame, drop repeats, drop blank 28, map `|` to space). Acceptance: the label reads \"this is an elicitation phrase to measure speech recognition performance\", 118 frames. This is \"tokens on an iPhone\".\n5. Replace the bundled features with the native frontend over the bundled PCM. Acceptance: identical transcript, max feature difference against the Mac features under 1e-3.\n6. Load the `fixed353` model with the CoreML EP (`MLProgram`, `RequireStaticInputShapes=1`, `ModelCacheDirectory` set to the app's cache). Acceptance: one partition, identical transcript, per-utterance time measured and recorded alongside first-launch and cached-launch session creation times. Mac reference: 10 ms inference, 19 s first compile.\n7. Pad the PCM with 0.5 s and 2 s of silence, re-run with matching fixed buckets. Acceptance: identical transcript. This answers the padding question before the grammar work starts.\n8. Record the numbers in the project docs and stop. Decoder and grammar are the next spike, and their Mac baselines are in this report (15 ms lexicon decode, under 1 ms command DFA).\n\n## Risk register\n\n| Risk | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| CoreML first-launch compile of a 111 MB MLProgram is slow on the phone | likely, bounded | `ModelCacheDirectory`; compile buckets in the background on first run; CPU EP meanwhile |\n| Silence padding to a bucket changes accuracy | possible | measured in spike step 7; shrink bucket spacing or fall back to CPU EP dynamic graph |\n| Grammar compiler gets a rule-call or LMWORD_CTX detail wrong | possible | the C++ is short and readable; test each construct against the Mac decoder first with ctypes, as done here |\n| Talon's Python rejection formula differs from the proposed path-vs-greedy check | likely but harmless | the margin is two orders of magnitude; tune on real audio |\n| Non-open captures (`<user.letter>` and similar) not exported by the feed | certain today | export their rules; see doc gaps |\n| EULA wording on use outside Talon | unknown | read it; email support@talonvoice.com; personal use only |\n| ORT CoreML EP behavior changes across versions | low | pin the pod version; keep the CPU EP path |\n\n## Recommendation\n\nRun the section 2 spike next: it is 2 to 3 days, needs only the four files in section 3 and an ORT pod, and turns \"demonstrated on the Mac\" into \"runs on the phone\" with the acceptance numbers above. Follow it with the decoder-plus-DFA spike (the Mac harness already exists in spirit: emissions file in, text out), then the grammar compiler, which is the largest and most Talon-specific piece. Keep Talon as the authority on the Mac and ship phrases through the existing `/execute` endpoint; do not b\n\nWork that should ship from this task: none in this repo as code. Two follow-ups are concrete: amend the prior report's conclusion, and export non-open capture definitions from the palette feed (doc gap 2).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"094ccb1c-2361-40cc-b849-4fdfb35dc0a2","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"3b59b3ab-40e2-4906-ae5b-518d2b1a7b53","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/trilliumsmith/fm_home/firstmate","sessionId":"3b59b3ab-40e2-4906-ae5b-518d2b1a7b53","version":"2.1.293","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floating-tinkering-lemur"}
